CREW: --for right now, Susan. So here's your three, two, one. And go ahead.

INT: Today is February 21, 2024. We are interviewing Frank Szirt. My name is Susan Kendal. And Frank is located in Burlington, Ontario. And we will be speaking in English.

CREW: OK. I'm going to go. We're rolling camera. And three, two, one--

INT: Good afternoon, Frank. It's so great to hear from you and to be with you. I'm wondering if you can just tell us your name, where you were born, and the year.

FS: My name is Frank Szirt, I was born in Budapest, Hungary, on September 18, 1938. And it is good to be with you.

INT: Thank you. If you could take us back to your earliest memories, what perhaps your home looked like or anything you want to share with us.

FS: My first memories are I may have been three or four years old. I remember-- I remember my father, who I lost in the war. But I was the apple of his eyes. I remember that. I remember him, on his-- in his spare time, taking me wherever I wanted to go. I have odd memories going around the streets of Budapest and walking into grocery stores and going by big barrels and pulling out sauerkraut and putting it in a paper and munching it as we were walking along the main streets of Budapest-- and remember playing dominoes with him. But I have very few other memories. My memory of my father was that he loved me. And the older I get, the more important it has become. And I guess I have more time to think about it. My memories of my mother, my first memories are sitting in her lap and listening to anti-aircraft weapons and listening to the roar of airplanes being-- and trying to-- not me, but my mother-- trying to identify whether they were German planes or Russian planes. Those were really my first memories. We lived, as I recall, in a one-bedroom apartment. And even then my father was ordered to forced labor. But he would come back from time to time.

Then I remember, in May-- and I almost remember the day. It was May 5-- but don't hold me to it-- where we were ordered to move into a Jewish house from our apartment. And I remember sitting-- our furniture was moved by a horse-drawn carriage. And I remember it was an exciting time for me. I didn't know what was happening, quite obviously. And I was sitting next to the man who drove the horses. And we ended up in a Jewish house. I remember the address. It was 48-A [?Legroty?] [?Karoi?] Street. And that's where we were until early November 1944.

INT: Can you describe what it was like then?

FS: I have very, very little memory of the time we spent in a Jewish house. Obviously, I didn't realize, I didn't understand what was happening. I didn't quite understand why we were there. But-- so I have actually very few memories of our stay. I do remember the day my parents were taken away. Oddly enough, I remember--

I could almost swear that it was November 6, 1944. And what happened was they took us down into the lobby of the apartment building, not just my parents and me, but other residents of the apartment building. And then they were taken away. And then here my memory stops. [PAUSES] To this day, I don't know why I wasn't taken. I can guess. I've inferred things, why I was not taken.

But I was not taken.

I went back to Budapest, not that I wanted to go back to Hungary because I didn't, but I wanted to show my wife, Lorraine, where I grew up. And I wanted to know if I-- because I remember the building. I remember the front of the building. And I remember the lamp that was at the top of the gate.

And this was in 2006. And I wandered in the neighborhood in and out of streets. And finally I noticed the lamp, which had 48-A on it. I walked over, and it was pretty much exactly the way I remembered what happened on November 6, 1944, except the lobby was a little bit smaller. And the street was a little bit narrower than I remembered. But other than that, I remember everything about the building itself.

INT: Do you remember how many people lived in your apartment or anything like that?

FS: It was a three-story apartment building-- [PAUSES] three or four stories. So I am guessing maybe 40 or 50 families. But there weren't that many people down in the lobby because it was a small lobby. So some of the people may have been taken before my parents were. That I don't know. My next memory is I was in an infamous brick factory in a suburb of Budapest. And the people who are still alive would remember it because that was kind of a dispatch point of the deportees. And I found myself there. Now, how I got from the apartment building to the brick factory, I don't know. Again, I can assume that it was the Red Cross. Someone from the Red Cross took me there. But I don't remember. I do remember being there for a couple of days. I remember sleep--

INT: How old were you?

FS: I was-- I had just turned six. I do remember sleeping on hay. And I remember that we were kind of ordered to stand around. And I remember one of the Nazis, one of the Arrow Cross guy-- men stuck one of the adults with a bayonet in the neck. And I remember a lady turning me away from it so that I wouldn't see it. Odd, the memories that stay, that stay with you. And then a couple-- either one or two days later, someone took me out of there. And I ended up what I assume was a Red Cross home. And again, I assume that the other kids who were my age, maybe a few younger, a few older, were all Jewish kids whose parents were deported.

Now, why I wasn't taken away is an interesting inference. And it is truly an inference. I read a couple of books about the siege of Budapest and, obviously, the role of Eichmann. But Eichmann had a second in command called Lieutenant Becher. And if you read Kastzner's Train, you'd run into this Becher character quite often. He and a fellow by the name of Kastzner, who was known as Rezso or Rudolf or Israel, continued to negotiate with Becher to allow a train full of Jews leave Budapest. And supposedly, Switzerland would accept them. As it turns out, I-- as I read later from another passenger of this train, who was-- who ended up being a professor of something or other in London, but he was on that train.

Who else-- and he wrote that the people on the train were, I believe, in Buchenwald for six months, in a concentration camp, because Switzerland was not very quick in accepting these people.

I'm kind of rambling on. A person who was-- whose name you may know was also on the train. And that's Peter Munk. Now, Peter, you may have heard of Peter Munk. Peter Munk ended up being the-- not the major shareholder, but certainly the mover and shaker behind, at one time, the biggest gold mine in the world. [PAUSES] Now, he died not that many years ago. And again, to talking about a small world. After my career, corporate career, I started consulting. And I was engaged by a lawyer for the Ontario Association for Architects. And that lawyer happened to be a stepbrother of Peter Munk's. And he was also on that train that Kastzner arranged. Getting back--

INT: So you were on the train?

FS: I was not. Oh, no, I was not on the train. No, no.

INT: OK.

FS: No, no. It was totally off my-- this is information that I picked up from books.

INT: OK.

FS: My take on why I wasn't deported or killed or managed to stay alive is that, in early November, Kastzner negotiated with Becher-- and that's B-E-C-H-E-R-- to provide some weaponry to the Nazis in return for allowing the train to go. And Becher, by then, knew that the Germans were losing the war. And he was concerned about his own future. And while Eichmann wanted to kill all Jews, Becher and Kastzner apparently agreed that children under a certain age would not be deported. This is not a fact. This is an inference on my part, that that's how I ended up staying alive. [PAUSES] My memories of the next two or three months are very faint. I recall one incident that I keep telling everybody because it's so unusual. You may know that the siege of Budapest involved basically the Soviet Army going from building to building to building. And we spent a fair amount of time in apartment building cellars, hoping to escape the bombardment. And I recall one time we were in downstairs in the cellar, and the bombing went on. And a Soviet-- either one or two Soviet soldiers ended up in our basement-- don't know how. And this guy put me on his lap and gave me two sugar cubes. This is something, you know, a memory I had never forgotten. But other than that, I don't remember a lot.

INT: Do you remember what you ate or what you did to keep busy?

FS: No, I don't remember what I ate. But again, when my aunt found me in late January 1945, I had not eaten probably anything for quite a while. And my aunt was told not to bother taking me because I was that close to death. And it was more than starvation. I mean, there was no food in Budapest. So there was-- I'm not quite sure, whatever little they fed me, where they found. Again, it's not something I can remember at all. I do remember my aunt dragging me home in kind of an inverted cupboard. There was snow on the ground. And I remember going by the eastern train station. So I had very, very little memories of that time. He took-- my aunt took me to her mother-in-law, my uncle by marriage, who was Gentile. And he was in the Hungarian Army. And a shrapnel found-- a shrapnel killed him. And that's when my aunt started looking for me. In any event, she took me to her mother in law's house. I remember the address where she was. And when we were back in Budapest in 2006, I showed Lorraine, this is where I was kind of saved. The lady was told by a doctor not to feed me very much because, obviously, I hadn't eaten. And a lot of food would not go well down with me. And--

INT: What year was this?

FS: I'm sorry.

INT: What year?

FS: This is January 1945.

INT: OK.

FS: So I'm still six years old. The-- on hearing that I shouldn't be fed a lot of food, my-- the lady said, well, if he's going to die, he's going to die on a full stomach. She cooked up two-- cabbage soup. I don't know where she found-- and there was meat in it. I assume horse meat. [PAUSES] But she fed it to me. I had to bowlfuls. And I ended up surviving.

I was also full of wounds. And I don't know whether this was a lack of hygiene or how that happened. I couldn't walk. And at this point, my mother is still not home. My mother is still in Lichtenworth, in a concentration camp.

So I had to learn how to walk again. But what I-- and again, going back and forth, what I didn't say before is that my mother and father were separated in a march to Austria. My mother ended up in Lichtenworth. There was a concentration camp. They were liberated by the Soviets either late January or early February. My father was in Felixdorf. I believe both of those towns are on the Southern part of Austria.

We know that my father was alive on January 31, 1945, because my mother worked for the Joint. I don't know if you know what the Joint is. It's the American Joint-- American Joint association. And they basically were set up to help Jews everywhere in the world. My mother ended up working for the Joint. And they had access to the list of deportees in Felixdorf. And so that's how we knew, or she knew, that he was alive on January 31. Again, I don't know where he died or how he died. I assume that they were-- that the Nazis were concerned about the approaching Soviets. And they decided to evacuate the camp and move them to another concentration camp. And of course, this was a forced march. And I assume that's where he died. So that's pretty much the early history of what I remember up to 1945. You know, from age three to six.

INT: Did you have any religion, any traditions that you recall from those early years?

FS: No. No. None. And that's because we didn't have any. That I am sure of. And we never-- my stepfather, later on, tried to teach me a little bit of Jewish history, but not from a religious-- not in a religious context. My parents and my stepfather were not religious. And so consequently, I grew up pretty much as a secular individual.

Between January and February and the time I went to grade one, again, I have very few memories. I do recall, though, that because of the wounds-- and basically, they were on my legs, my feet. And they-- the doctors then couldn't seem to cure it. And one doctor basically said that we need to amputate both legs. And at that time, my mother hadn't been home yet. And my aunt, God bless her, said, I will not take that responsibility on. And so it didn't happen. Subsequently, in the summer of 1945, my mother found a doctor who had access to penicillin. And penicillin was new then. And with the penicillin, my-- the wounds were cured in two weeks. So thank you, Aunt. And, you know, I've been pretty healthy since.

Grade one to eight, not much to say. [PAUSES] Obviously, we lived under the Soviet regime from 19-- I guess-- '45 on.

INT: Did you experience antisemitism?

FS: Well, that's a totally different topic. And we talk about antisemitism. Yes, I-- if-- and this is a general comment-- if you are a Jew, and whether you believe it or not, and regardless of where you live, I learned that you are always "the other." Regardless-- and that applies to Canada. It applies to the US. It's just the way the world is. That's pretty much my opinion. And I'm pretty convinced that I am-- that it's a correct opinion. Yes, there was a-- Hungary is-- was much more of an antisemitic-- obviously, Hungary was antisemitic between the two world wars, obviously. And that's what gave rise to the Arrow Cross, which was [PAUSES] the equivalent of the SS. Only, they were even-- if it's possible, they were even more cruel than the SS. And if you interviewed-- if you interviewed any Hungarian Jews, they might have mentioned that the Arrow Cross would line up Jews on the bank of the Danube and shot them into the water, into the Danube River. And these-- you know, it went on in the war. But in a silent way, in a-- under the surface, there was antisemitism. [PAUSES] And I knew that I was the other, as were my Jewish friends. And most of my friends were, in fact, Jewish. [PAUSES] Antisemitism, If we talk about antisemitism today, I am not a prophet, but I've predicted that antisemitism would rise again because it's not something that after 2,000 years all of a sudden is going to disappear. And so I am not shocked that we are kind of approaching, not the same level of antisemitism, but certainly a lot more than I was hoping for when I crossed the Atlantic. I was hoping that I would not-- I would not encounter it so openly again.

So that was the eight years, '45 to '53. '53, I wanted to go to a high school, a gymnasium, what in Hungary was called a gymnasium, which was a standard high school. Despite the fact that I had the best marks in public school, I was not allowed because my mother wasn't part of the working class. She was a typist. I don't know if you know what a typist is. She was a typist who typed for other people. But that was not considered to be part of the working class. And so I couldn't go to the high school that I wanted to go. I ended up going to a technical school, thank goodness, in hindsight, called the Technical School for Building Materials. I went there for three years. I was in my fourth year when a Hungarian uprising broke out. And as soon as I knew that the border was open, I decided to leave, to basically escape.

As it turned out, I escaped with who then was my girlfriend and her family. And I crossed the Hungarian-Austrian border in November, I believe November 4 or 6, 1956.

INT: Did your mother join you?

FS: No, my parents didn't. I had my mother and my stepfather then. My mother remarried in the late '40s. And so my stepfather basically is responsible-- was responsible for bringing me up. And while he was not a very warm man, he was, thankfully, pretty strict. And so I ended up kind of on the straight and narrow. If it hadn't been for him, I don't know where I would have ended up. So I didn't-- they didn't come with us, A, because they were not all that well, physically. And B, because my stepfather's brother lived in Australia. My stepfather's brother and mother, they lived in Australia. And they kind of committed to bring them out. As it turned out, they backed out of it. And my stepfather died in 1964. My mother stayed alive. I brought my mother out in 1964. By then, I was married. But she couldn't get used to Canada, and so she went back a year and a half later. And she passed away in 1996 at the age of 86. [BACKGROUND NOISE]

So in Austria, we were offered three options. Austria was overloaded with Hungarians. In a very short time, 200,000 Hungarians ended up in Austria. And Austria is not that big a country. And obviously, you know, they had just recovered from the Soviet, kind of, occupation. They weren't loaded with funds to support that many people. So we were offered an opportunity to go either to England, Italy, or France.

The family I was with and I chose England. And we ended up in England on-- in November, late November 1956. [PAUSES] Yes, 1956. And then--

INT: Who was the family you were with?

FS: The family was a Hungarian family. They had relatives in Cleveland. And they ended up in Cleveland. And in the fullness of time, we lost touch. It was just that I had met this girl the previous summer. And her mother, when they were ready to leave, they wanted to-- they said, do you want to come with us? And I said, sure. And so we ended up going. My girlfriend had a sister and a mother. So the four of us were together-- mother, two daughters, and me. But in England, we ended up separating because they were waiting to be accepted by the US. And of course, I wanted to go to the US too. Candidly, I didn't know where Canada was. And I was convinced that Niagara Falls was in Africa. So geography, obvious, not my strongest subject. And of course, I thought they said, why don't you go to Canada? It's next door to the US.

They took us down from Birmingham, which is where I was with a family, to the Canadian Embassy. I don't know. We signed papers, and two weeks later I was on my way to Canada. Thank goodness.

INT: How did you find that family that you lived with?

FS: They-- I guess the Red Cross or whoever looked after the refugees were assigning individuals or families to English families who were ready to take on some Hungarians. And I think there was some payment by the Red Cross or whoever sponsored this. That's how we ended up with these families. And that was really an opportunity for me to learn English to the-- as well as I speak now. [LAUGHING] And-- but in any event, in May of 1957, I arrived in Canada. Actually, it was May 12, 1957.

INT: How did you get to Canada?

FS: They flew us into-- we landed in Gander. From Gander, they flew us into Winnipeg. And those of us who wanted to come to Toronto were put on a train. And that was a two-day train trip back to Toronto. And ever since then, I've been in Toronto and the GTA.

INT: Do you remember anything about the trip?

FS: Well, it was a Flying Tiger airplane, which people familiar with the Second World War, it rang a bell. But that was the first time I flew. And I don't remember very much, obviously. The train trip coming back to Toronto, I do remember going by the Gardiner Expressway because, obviously, we went by it. And I remember, I'd never seen that many cars in my life. That's kind of my memory of my arrival to Toronto. In Toronto, I guess we were in a Red Cross residence. It was previously a religious building, maybe a nunnery, off Queen Street West.

And that's where-- that's where we were. We did not get the kind of reception-- and this is not a complaint. We did not get the kind of receptions that current refugees get. We were basically told to get out of there and get a job. And so we, first thing we did was walked around and got a job. And I was very fortunate. I got a job with a company called Art Bookbinding. This was on McCaul Street. And McCaul street, you may know or you may not, is one street west of University. Anyway, it was owned by a Jewish family. And they happened to have a room to rent because they had just bought a home. And they had a room to rent. And so I moved in there. And I lived with them for until I got married. And they kind of became my surrogate parents. And in fact, when I got married, it was the Cosman family, the family I lived with, that gave me away. So I really lucked out when I arrived in Canada and Toronto.

INT: What do you remember about it?

FS: About?

INT: Arriving.

FS: Arriving to Toronto?

INT: Mhm.

FS: Well, I always liked streetcars. [PAUSES] And in fact, one of the things I did with my dad is-- with my father-- is that I used to ask him to take me on a streetcar ride. And we would take the Number 6 streetcar, which was-- had the longest route. And so wherever I went-- and even now, whenever I-- we did a fair amount of traveling-- if there was a streetcar, the first thing I-- or a subway-- the first thing I did was I found it and I got on it. So I remember the streetcar. At that time, Queen Street, which is where I spent the first couple of days looking for work, was very much almost like a village street-- low-rise buildings, stores along the street. On Sundays, you could shoot a cannon down the middle of the street and wouldn't hit anything. So that was my first impression. And obviously, over the last number of years it's changed a lot and changed for the better. But those were my first impressions. There were very few highrise buildings then.

But that was pretty much my impression. I was glad to be here. I can tell you that. I was glad to be away from Europe. And I actually almost swore that I would never go back. And the only time I went back was when I took Lorraine back just to show her where I grew up. But I would never have-- and I'm talking about continental Europe specifically, because I've been to Britain quite a few times. But I have no desire ever to go back to continental Europe.

INT: And when you arrived, were you able to speak English?

FS: I was able to make myself understood. And I understood some of it. But it was fairly quick that I picked it up. And I needed to pick it up because, when I left Hungary, I was in my last year of high school. And when I left Budapest, I promised-- my mother made me promise that I would finish university. Well, when I left Hungary, I hadn't even finished high school. So I needed to finish high school. But I also needed to work. So I actually ended up finishing high school partially by myself and partially by night school, but mostly by myself. And the one thing the Communist regime was good at was education. And so a lot of the great 13 topics, certainly in the sciences-- chemistry, physics, the three maths-- I knew most of it. And so I was able to pick it up-- pick them up pretty quickly. And I ended up-- it took me two years, but I finished my grade 13. Finished English and the two languages because, in Hungary, we were-- their compulsory language was Russian. The University of Toronto accommodated me and allowed me to write an English-- I'm sorry, a Russian literature, Russian grammar exam, which I passed. I don't know whether I passed or not. They gave me a passing mark. And so in 1960, I-- the U of T accepted me for the, what they called was the extension department. And so I started university in 1960. In 1960, the only course that you could take in the evening was a general BA. So I took a general BA. And I finished university in 1965 while working full time. And by then, I had a daughter. So I fulfilled my promise to my mom.

The education itself, I majored in philosophy. I can tell you, it did me no apparent good, except that I was able to get a job because of the university. Because the company that I-- that hired me in 1965 was an American company. And for the professional jobs, they would only hire university graduates. And so I was able to-- I qualified as a university graduate. And that's how I ended up in the insurance business. [PAUSES] Now, the insurance company was the Travelers. There-- they, since then, left Canada and returned. So the Travelers is still in business, although it's probably a little different from the one I worked at. But that's where I basically learned underwriting. Now, I'm asked what my occupation is. Well, my occupation is underwriting, underwriter. But that doesn't mean that those were the jobs that I always had. I guess I progressed over the years. And my last corporate job, I was the president and chief executive officer of an American company, a small American-- well, the American company is big. The Canadian branch was small. And I ended up running it between 1998 and 2003. So I am an underwriter. Most of my work in the past was underwriting. But I occupied different positions.

INT: And did you experience any antisemitism?

FS: Not at work. And one of the things about the Travelers was that I truly believe that was not an antisemitic company. They were so far ahead of the corporate world in many, many respects, the way they treated their employees. They were-- that was a tremendous, a tremendous company. I don't know if it's still the same. But to show you how great, how really great it was, I still have two friends from the Travelers that we worked together 50-some years ago. The way they hired, they did most of the hiring by the people you would be working with. Certainly somebody made a final decision, but pretty much all the interviewing took place by your future colleagues. It was a tremendous company. But no, I didn't. And did I experience overt antisemitism in my career? No. No, I did not.

INT: And when you arrived in Canada, did you experience any difficulties because you were an immigrant?

FS: If I did, I chose to forget it. I don't think I did. But I was so happy to be here that maybe I understood that I'm here. Maybe I don't have the same rights as people who were born-- I don't know what was in the back of my mind. This is thinking, really, of hindsight. I lost the presidency of one company. And some people suggested that this was because I was an immigrant. I chose not to believe that. Maybe it was the fact that the company was looking for a different person than I was. And I was quite OK with that. So, no, I-- no, I truly don't think I was ever-- [PAUSES] I ever had-- I never-- I don't think I ever had any disadvantage because of my immigrant status.

INT: And you said you were married and you had children?

FS: I got married on December 24, 1961. And the reason it was December 24 was because I only had a-- it was a Sunday. I got married in the Beth Sholom synagogue-- I don't know if it's still there or not-- on Eglinton Avenue. And the rabbi who married us was Rabbi David Monson. I still remember that.

The reason we got married late December, because I had one week between the fall season and the spring term at university. And so I couldn't miss. I couldn't miss any class because, you know, they were basically once a week, three hours. So I couldn't miss classes. And I still have a-- and I didn't bring it up, but I still have a photograph my wife took lying down in the-- on our honeymoon in-- you know, in a hotel, and reading a book on English history. I was studying for a term test that was supposed to happen on January 2. Anyway. So we got married. Our first daughter was born on September 10, 1962. And her name was Michelle. [PAUSES] Then I continued on. university, continued working.

Our second daughter, Anita, was born on May 9. [PAUSES] And May 8 was the time I wrote my last university exam in 1965. So I do remember the-- which was a Friday, and Anita was born on May 9.

And my youngest daughter, Gayle, was born in September, on September 12, 1967. So we had three birthdays in September, very close by. Lorraine was born on May 10, '42. So May was a busy time too. We had Anita's birthday, Lorraine's birthday, and Mother's Day. And incidentally, Anita and Lorraine both were born on Mother's Day, only a day apart.

INT: And did you choose to raise your children with Jewish traditions?

FS: No. No. I regret it. I did not. And part of the reason is that I was born in Jewish tradition, and I didn't quite know what Jewish tradition was. We did-- we did do Passover dinners at the Seligmans' at [PAUSES] Renee's home. And-- but generally speaking, no. I did not raise them in a Jewish tradition. And I regret it now.

INT: Did you have a relationship with God at all?

FS: No. No. No. I am-- you can call me agnostic. [PAUSES] I won't call myself an atheist because I don't know. And that, I think, is what agnostic means, not knowing. [PAUSES] You know, so far no one's figured out how the world started, how the universe started. And I've watched a bunch of shows on the start and the development of the universe. And [PAUSES] no one's been able to figure out how. I mean, they talk about a Big Bang, but there was something before the Big Bang. And my mind simply can't understand the concept of nothing any more than my mind can understand the concept of infinity. So I am an agnostic. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

Now if you--

INT: And--

FS: I'd be happy to talk about the stuff that does not relate to work.

I love the theater. And we used to go to Stratford and Niagara-on-the-Lake quite regularly. I love the opera. We had subscription tickets to Opera Hamilton while it lasted. And in terms of-- in terms of the physical exercise, [PAUSES] I did nothing until the mid 40s. And in mid 40s, I took up running. And I became totally addicted to running. And I ended up running-- well, I had friends, but we ran for 30 or more years six days a week, and for a period of 10 years, two marathons a year. So I ran 15 marathons between 1960-- 1986 and 1994. The only marathon that I wanted to run but did not was Boston because while I loved running, I was lousy at it. And I could never qualify for Boston. For the other marathons, you didn't have to qualify. You just have to-- you just had to pay $30 or $40 and show up. So that was my-- that was basically most of my recreation. And I made some very good friends running.

So that's pretty much-- [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] sorry, I should tell you--

INT: In terms of antisemitism--

FS: What would you like to-- what would you like me to say? It's 2,000 years old, two millennia-- two millennia old. [PAUSES] I have basically given up. I gave up a long time ago, that it would ever disappear. It may fluctuate. It may become more intense, deeper, or it may kind of subside for a while. But it will never disappear. And as I said before, [PAUSES] in my opinion, a Jew is-- a Jewish person is not at home anywhere except in Israel.

That is an opinion I formed a few years ago. And so far, nothing happened to dissuade me from it. And that's, incidentally, why I am so worried about Israel, because Israel has to be there for the diaspora to go somewhere when antisemitism ignites again. And it will-- [PAUSES] hopefully not in my life, hopefully not in my children and grandchildren's lives. The only good thing is-- and I keep telling them that because my grandchildren have Gentile [PAUSES] fathers, but because of the Jewish tradition, the mother is Jewish, they can move to Israel pretty much anytime. And so I have my father's birth certificate. And I will leave it to them as proof that they are, in fact, Jewish.

But that's why I am so worried that this constant warring will end up, again, dissuading people to live there because nobody-- no one wants to live in a constant state of war or readiness for war.

Again, that's my opinion. I-- [PAUSES] and if you're interested in my political views, I don't believe in a two-state system. Because for two states, you need two states. And so far, there is only one state. And the other state doesn't want to recognize the other state. So I'd like to ask the proponents of two-state, of a two-state solution, name the two states. [LAUGHING] And good luck to them.

INT: And what do you think about Germany now and their response to antisemitism? Did you ever see that coming in your lifetime?

